  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two reconstructions of Earth in the Late Paleozoic, emphasizing a major climate shift from a warm Devonian world at about 375 million years ago to an icehouse world by about 320 million years ago. Both maps show ancient continental positions and use icons to summarize large-scale environmental processes.\n\nIn the older Devonian panel, the planet appears broadly warm and wet, with strong monsoon belts marked across tropical landmasses and circular arrows over the oceans indicating active circulation and moisture cycling. No large continental ice is shown, suggesting an overall greenhouse or “warmhouse” state.\n\nIn the younger Mississippian–Pennsylvanian panel, the scene becomes much more complex and colder. Monsoons are still present, but wildfire symbols and several “CO2” icons indicate widespread organic carbon burial on land, while “CH4” and dotted ground symbols mark permafrost development. Large snowflake symbols over southern high-latitude continents and sea-ice icons around polar oceans show substantial continental and marine ice expansion. The overall message is that Earth transitioned from a relatively ice-free, monsoon-dominated warm climate to a glaciated icehouse system with carbon drawdown, permafrost, and expanded polar ice.",
